# `if (list[i & 7] > 3) .. else if (list[i & 7] > 1) ..`: the second read is answered by what the first has found. Where proofs are checked at run time such a read is built once more, without the answers of the state, to hold memory against what was remembered:
# behind a row in front of the loop that read would test for itself, and the look at the loop has counted on no other code. It is left out there
# It was "an access in a loop does what the look at the loop has not counted on"
